    parentName: "p"
  }, "immensely difficult"), ".  Having so many different linguistic groups with differing needs packed into a relatively tight area (at least on a global scale) means that the solution is not as simple as \"better NLP tools\" or \"better cross-cultural collaboration\" or \"better linguistic analysis\" but is usually a combination of those factors and more."), mdx("p", null, "(As another side note going beyond what the paper says, I also encountered a tricky situation where one cultural group in Indonesia that I visited had some members that did not want another cultural group to receive NLP development.  I am not going to name either group, as I do not want to put them in a bad light, as I certainly do not believe that the entirety of the former group feels this way.  Yet this goes to show that the cultural side of things can play a HUGE role in determining the effectiveness of inclusive research practices - and navigating that can be a huge challenge.)"), mdx("h4", {
